{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.12","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":31328,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Step 1: The Blueprint (Power Analysis)\n\n**The Concept:** You wouldn't launch a rocket without knowing how much fuel you need. In A/B testing, Power Analysis tells us how much \"fuel\" (sample size / number of users) we need to actually detect a difference between our Control and Treatment groups, assuming a difference actually exists.\n\nIf you don't calculate this beforehand, you might run a test with too few users, see no difference, and falsely conclude the treatment failed (a Type II error).\n\nTo calculate this, we balance three levers:\n1. Effect Size (Cohen's d): How big of a difference do we expect to see? (Small = 0.2, Medium = 0.5, Large = 0.8). Smaller effects require more users to detect.\n2. Alpha ($\\alpha$): Our false positive rate, usually set to 0.05 (5%).\n3. Power: Our true positive rate, usually set to 0.80 (80%). This means we want an 80% chance of detecting a real effect.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-16T12:08:44.512616Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-16T12:08:44.513385Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-16T12:08:45.005831Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-16T12:08:44.513344Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-16T12:08:45.004576Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def calculate_sample_size(effect_size, alpha=0.05, power=0.80):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the required sample size per group for an A/B test.\n    \"\"\"\n    analysis = TTestIndPower()\n\n    # ratio=1.0 means we want equal sample sizes for Control and Treatment\n    sample_size = analysis.solve_power(\n        effect_size = effect_size,\n        power = power,\n        alpha = alpha,\n        ratio = 1.0\n    )\n\n    # We round up because we can't have a fraction of a user\n    return int(np.ceil(sample_size))\n\nn_required = calculate_sample_size(effect_size=0.2)\nprint(f\"We need {n_required} users per group!\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-16T12:37:51.496211Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-16T12:37:51.496577Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-16T12:37:51.509335Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-16T12:37:51.496547Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-16T12:37:51.508402Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"We need 394 users per group!\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":5},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Step 2: Simulating the Data\nIn a real scenario, you'd pull this data from a SQL database or a log file. Since we are building the pipeline from scratch, we are going to simulate our users. We will generate two sets of normal distributions: one for Control, one for Treatment.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def simulate_ab_data(n, mean_control, std_control, mean_treatment, std_treatment):\n    \"\"\"\n    Simulates normally distributed continuous data (e.g., revenue per user, time on page) \n    for Control and Treatment groups.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Setting a random seed ensures we get the same \"random\" numbers every time we run it. \n    np.random.seed(42)\n\n    # Generate data for the Control and Treatment group\n    control = np.random.normal(loc=mean_control, scale=std_control, size=n)\n    treatment = np.random.normal(loc=mean_treatment, scale=std_treatment, size=n)\n\n    return control, treatment\n\nn_users = n_required\n\ncontrol_group, treatment_group = simulate_ab_data(\n    n = n_users,\n    mean_control = 100,\n    std_control = 15,\n    mean_treatment = 105,\n    std_treatment = 15\n)\n\nprint(f\"Simulated data for {len(control_group)} users in Control.\")\nprint(f\"Control Mean: {np.mean(control_group):.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Treatment Mean: {np.mean(treatment_group):.2f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-16T12:39:47.203410Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-16T12:39:47.204206Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-16T12:39:47.212302Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-16T12:39:47.204173Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-16T12:39:47.211031Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Simulated data for 394 users in Control.\nControl Mean: 100.29\nTreatment Mean: 104.53\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":6},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Step 3: Practical Significance (Cohen's d)\n\n**The Concept:** There is a massive difference between Statistical Significance (is the difference real?) and Practical Significance (does the difference matter?).\n\nIf you have millions of users, a t-test might tell you that a \\$$0.01 increase in revenue is \"statistically significant\" (meaning it's definitely not a fluke). But a \\$$0.01 increase might not even cover the engineering cost of deploying the new feature!\n\nCohen’s d measures practical significance. It tells you the size of the effect by standardizing it.\n\n1. **~0.2** = Small effect (Might be worth it, depending on volume)\n\n2.  **~0.5** = Medium effect (Solid improvement)\n\n3. **~0.8+** = Large effect (Massive win!)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def calculate_cohens_d(control, treatment):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates Cohen's d for independent samples.\n    Formula: (Mean of Treatment - Mean of Control) / Pooled Standard Deviation\n    \"\"\"\n    n1, n2 = len(control), len(treatment)\n\n    # Calculate the variance of each group (ddof=1 is for sample variance)\n    var1 = np.var(control, ddof=1)\n    var2 = np.var(treatment, ddof=1)\n\n    # Calculate \"pooled\" standard deviation\n    # This is essentially the average spread of the two groups combined\n    pooled_std = np.sqrt(((n1-1) * var1 + (n2-1) * var2)/(n1+n2-2))\n\n    # Calculate Cohen's d\n    d = (np.mean(treatment)-np.mean(control))/pooled_std\n\n    return d\n\nactual_d = calculate_cohens_d(control_group, treatment_group)\n\nprint(f\"Observed Cohen's d: {actual_d:.2f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-16T12:57:57.947798Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-16T12:57:57.948658Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-16T12:57:57.955905Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-16T12:57:57.948620Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-16T12:57:57.954876Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Observed Cohen's d: 0.29\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":7},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Step 4: Welch's T-Test and Confidence Intervals\n**The Concept:** We are going to use a Welch's T-Test. Why Welch's instead of a standard Student's T-Test? Because standard t-tests assume the variance (spread) of both groups is exactly identical. In the real world, this is rarely true. Welch's t-test is safer and more robust because it doesn't make that assumption.\n\n* **P-value:** This will tell us the probability of seeing this 0.29 difference if the Treatment actually had no effect at all (the null hypothesis). If p < 0.05, we reject the null hypothesis and call it statistically significant.\n\n* **Confidence Interval (CI):** The p-value just says \"yes\" or \"no\". The CI gives us a range. It says: \"We are 95% confident that the true difference between the groups lies between [Lower Bound] and [Upper Bound].\"","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def perform_ttest_and_ci(control, treatment, alpha=0.05):\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs Welch's t-test and calculates the 95% Confidence Interval.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # 1. Welch's t-test \n    # equal_var=False is what makes this a Welch's test instead of a standard Student's t-test!\n    t_test, p_value = stats.ttest_ind(treatment, control, equal_var=False)\n\n    # 2. Confidence Interval calculation\n    mean_diff = np.mean(treatment) - np.mean(control)\n\n    # Calculate degrees of freedom for Welch's test\n    var_c, var_t = np.var(control, ddof=1), np.var(treatment, ddof=1)\n    n_c, n_t = len(control), len(treatment)\n    df = ((var_c/n_c + var_t/n_t)**2) / ((var_c/n_c)**2 / (n_c-1) + (var_t/n_t)**2 / (n_t-1))\n\n    # Find the critical t-value\n    t_crit = stats.t.ppf(1-alpha/2, df)\n\n    # Calculate the margin of error\n    margin_of_error = t_crit * np.sqrt(var_c/n_c + var_t/n_t)\n\n    ci_lower = mean_diff - margin_of_error\n    ci_upper = mean_diff + margin_of_error\n\n    return p_value, mean_diff, (ci_lower, ci_upper)\n    \np_val, mean_difference, conf_interval = perform_ttest_and_ci(control_group, treatment_group)\n\nprint(f\"Mean Difference: {mean_difference:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"P-Value: {p_val:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"95% Confidence Interval: [{conf_interval[0]:.2f}, {conf_interval[1]:.2f}]\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-16T13:38:40.734350Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-16T13:38:40.735304Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-16T13:38:40.746216Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-16T13:38:40.735253Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-16T13:38:40.745399Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Mean Difference: 4.24\nP-Value: 0.0001\n95% Confidence Interval: [2.17, 6.31]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We have built all the individual Lego blocks:\n\n* calculate_sample_size (The Fuel)\n\n* simulate_ab_data (The Users)\n\n* calculate_cohens_d (Practical Significance / The Treasure Size)\n\n* perform_ttest_and_ci (Statistical Significance / The Bouncer & Safety Net)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Step 5: The Master Wrapper\nThe prompt you were given asked to \"Combine both implementations... Wrap in clean functions.\"\n\nIn Data Science and Machine Learning Engineering, you don't want to run these blocks one by one every time you test a new predictive model. If you are rolling out a new XGBoost model to replace an older Logistic Regression baseline, you want a single pipeline that spits out a clear business recommendation.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def run_full_ab_test(expected_effect_size, mean_c, std_c, mean_t, std_t, alpha=0.05, power=0.80):\n    \"\"\"\n    Runs the entire A/B testing pipeline from planning to interpretation.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"=== 🚀 A/B Test Execution Pipeline ===\\n\")\n    \n    # Phase 1: Planning\n    n_required = calculate_sample_size(expected_effect_size, alpha, power)\n    print(f\"[Planning] Required sample size per group: {n_required}\")\n    \n    # Phase 2: Data Collection (Simulated)\n    control, treatment = simulate_ab_data(n_required, mean_c, std_c, mean_t, std_t)\n    print(f\"[Data] Collected {n_required} observations per group.\\n\")\n    \n    # Phase 3: Calculation\n    actual_d = calculate_cohens_d(control, treatment)\n    p_value, mean_diff, ci = perform_ttest_and_ci(control, treatment, alpha)\n\n    # Phase 4: Interpretation\n    print(\"=== 📊 Results & Interpretation ===\")\n    print(f\"Observed Mean Difference: {mean_diff:.2f}\")\n    print(f\"95% Confidence Interval:  [{ci[0]:.2f}, {ci[1]:.2f}]\")\n    print(f\"P-Value:                  {p_value:.4f}\")\n    print(f\"Cohen's d (Effect Size):  {actual_d:.2f}\\n\")\n    \n    print(\"=== 💡 Final Conclusion ===\")\n\n    # 1. Check Statistical Significance (Is the Confidence Interval positive?)\n    if p_value<alpha:\n        print(f\"✅ Statistical: Significant (p < {alpha}). The difference is real, not just noise.\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"❌ Statistical: NOT significant (p >= {alpha}). We cannot prove the treatment worked.\")\n        \n    # 2. Check Practical Significance (Does the size of the difference matter?)\n    if abs(actual_d)>0.2:\n        mag = \"Negligible\"\n    elif abs(actual_d) < 0.5:\n        mag = \"Small\"\n    elif abs(actual_d) < 0.8:\n        mag = \"Medium\"\n    else:\n        mag = \"Large\"\n        \n    print(f\"📏 Practical: The effect size is considered '{mag}'.\\n\")\n    \n    # 3. Final Business Recommendation\n    if p_value < alpha and abs(actual_d) >= 0.2:\n        print(\"🟢 RECOMMENDATION: Deploy the new treatment. It is mathematically reliable and practically meaningful.\")\n    elif p_value < alpha and abs(actual_d) < 0.2:\n        print(\"🟡 RECOMMENDATION: The result is mathematically real, but the actual impact is negligible. Weigh deployment costs carefully before shipping.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"🔴 RECOMMENDATION: Do not deploy. The new treatment did not show a reliable improvement.\")\n\n# --- Execute the Pipeline ---\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Let's say we expect a small-to-medium effect (0.3). \n    # Control historically has a mean of 100 (std 15). \n    # The new treatment scores slightly higher at 105.\n    run_full_ab_test(\n        expected_effect_size=0.3, \n        mean_c=100, std_c=15, \n        mean_t=105, std_t=15\n    )","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-16T14:04:34.131327Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-16T14:04:34.131677Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-16T14:04:34.152577Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-16T14:04:34.131646Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-16T14:04:34.151698Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"=== 🚀 A/B Test Execution Pipeline ===\n\n[Planning] Required sample size per group: 176\n[Data] Collected 176 observations per group.\n\n=== 📊 Results & Interpretation ===\nObserved Mean Difference: 6.50\n95% Confidence Interval:  [3.49, 9.50]\nP-Value:                  0.0000\nCohen's d (Effect Size):  0.45\n\n=== 💡 Final Conclusion ===\n✅ Statistical: Significant (p < 0.05). The difference is real, not just noise.\n📏 Practical: The effect size is considered 'Negligible'.\n\n🟢 RECOMMENDATION: Deploy the new treatment. It is mathematically reliable and practically meaningful.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}